 ## Overview
-Triple Plane (TP) Decomposition is a tensor factorization method for 3D scene representation that replaces the 1D vectors of standard Canonical Polyadic (CP) decomposition with 2D factor matrices (planes) [S1]. It solves the problem of high computational cost and poor representation quality associated with the extremely high ranks required by vector-based CP decomposition for complex scenes [S1]. Unlike its sibling [Decomposed Low-Rank Tensor (VM-Decomposition)](7116-decomposed-low-rank-tensor-vm-decomposition.md), which relaxes low-rank constraints for specific modes, TP decomposition uniformly adopts 2D structures across all three spatial axes to trade storage compactness for computational efficiency and improved quality at lower ranks [S1].
+Triple Plane (TP) Decomposition is a tensor factorization method for 3D scene representation that replaces the 1D vectors of standard Canonical Polyadic (CP) decomposition with 2D factor matrices (planes) [S1]. It solves the problem of high computational cost and poor representation quality associated with the extremely high ranks required by vector-based CP decomposition for complex scenes [S1]. Unlike its sibling [Decomposed Low-Rank Tensor (VM-Decomposition)](7116-decomposed-low-rank-tensor-vm-decomposition.md), which relaxes low-rank constraints for specific modes, TP decomposition adopts 2D structures across all three spatial axes to trade storage compactness for computational efficiency and improved quality at lower ranks [S1]. A hybrid variant (HY) further allows selecting vector- or matrix-based decomposition independently for each rank [S1].
 
